Bubble Sort is inherently inefficient for large datasets, but it can be optimized in a few ways. Here are some key optimizations:

### 1. **Stop Early If Already Sorted (Best Case: O(n))**
   - If no swaps occur in a full pass, the array is already sorted. Introduce a `swapped` flag to check this.

   ```cpp
   void optimizedBubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
       bool swapped;
       for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
           swapped = false;
           for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
               if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                   std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
                   swapped = true;
               }
           }
           if (!swapped) break; // Stop if no swaps happened
       }
   }
   ```

### 2. **Reduce Unnecessary Comparisons**
   - The largest element moves to the correct position in each pass. So, reduce the loop range after each pass.

   ```cpp
   void optimizedBubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
       int lastSwapped; 
       int boundary = n - 1; 
       do {
           lastSwapped = 0;
           for (int i = 0; i < boundary; i++) {
               if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                   std::swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                   lastSwapped = i; // Track last swap position
               }
           }
           boundary = lastSwapped; // Reduce the range of sorting
       } while (lastSwapped > 0);
   }
   ```

### 3. **Bidirectional (Cocktail Shaker Sort)**
   - Bubble Sort only moves elements in one direction. A bidirectional version can improve performance in nearly sorted cases.

   ```cpp
   void cocktailSort(int arr[], int n) {
       bool swapped = true;
       int start = 0, end = n - 1;
       while (swapped) {
           swapped = false;
           for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
               if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                   std::swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                   swapped = true;
               }
           }
           if (!swapped) break;
           swapped = false;
           end--;
           for (int i = end - 1; i >= start; i--) {
               if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                   std::swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                   swapped = true;
               }
           }
           start++;
       }
   }
   ```

### 4. **Use Bubble Sort Only When Necessary**
   - If sorting is needed for small datasets (≤ 10 elements) or nearly sorted data, Bubble Sort can be okay.
   - For larger datasets, prefer **Quick Sort, Merge Sort, or Heap Sort**, which have better average-case performance.

