### **Time Complexity Analysis**
| Case        | Complexity |
|------------|------------|
| **Best Case (Already Sorted)** | **O(n²)** |
| **Worst Case (Reversed Order)** | **O(n²)** |
| **Average Case** | **O(n²)** |

- Selection Sort **always** runs in **O(n²)** because it compares every element even if already sorted.
- It performs **(n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 1 ≈ n²/2 comparisons**.

### **Space Complexity**
- **O(1)** (Only a few extra variables, no extra memory used)

---

### **Final Notes**
✅ **Advantages**
- Simple to implement.
- Works well for **small arrays**.

❌ **Disadvantages**
- Slow for **large datasets**.
- Doesn't take advantage of partially sorted data.

If you're sorting large datasets, better alternatives include:
- **Quick Sort (O(n log n))**
- **Merge Sort (O(n log n))**
- **Heap Sort (O(n log n))**

